# ESP32 family: source-level portability

Product target today is **ESP32-S3**. The kernel, apps, and Argon CC stay **one tree** for the whole Espressif family. A different chip is a rebuild (`idf.py set-target`, `mkaxe.py --arch xtensa|riscv32`), not a fork and not one binary. Pico / Pi / Luckfox / x86 are out of scope.

Do not start a multi-target port unless asked. When a change already touches the kernel, ABI, CC, or app build, do not make the S3/Xtensa knot worse.

- Branch on **capability** (`SOC_*`, `ag_platform()`, `full`/`lite`/`nano`, core count, executable PSRAM, USB host) — not on `CONFIG_IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3` or chip names in app logic.
- New `<xtensa/...>` or a hard `xtensa` CMake require: wrap with `#ifdef __XTENSA__` (RISC-V C3/C6/P4 must still be thinkable).
- A feature missing on part of the family compiles out or degrades; it must not break the build.
- Same app `.c` for every target; ISA is the toolchain. An `.AXE` is native — rebuild it, do not expect Xtensa images on RISC-V.
- Argon CC: one frontend; Xtensa vs RISC-V are backends.
- `BOARD.CFG` selects a board for **one** SoC. It is not a chip switch.

```c
/* bad */  #if CONFIG_IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3
/* good */ if (ag_platform()->psram_executable)
```
